This guide explains how to create a simple trait mod for [[Project Zomboid]], explaining the various steps involved in detail:
#Registration
#Trait script
#Referencing the trait in Lua

The creation of a trait mod involves the definition of this trait in the first place, but the implementation of what this trait does needs to be programmed in [[Lua]], which this guide won't explain in-depth since there is an infinite possibility of functionality which could be implemented for traits.

== Registration ==
{{Main|Registries}}
You must first [[Registries|register]] the trait in the trait registry. This creates a key to reference the trait by. Start by creating the registry file (<code>media/registries.lua</code>) in your mod with the following content:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
---FILE: media/registries.lua

-- replace with name of the global you created to store your registries above
_MyModRegistries = {} -- this is a table, it will hold references to your registered elements

-- we store your trait registries in a subtable of the global table
-- so it is not mixed with other registries you may add to your mod
_MyModRegistries.traits = {
    -- "mymod:mytrait" is the identifier for this trait
    -- "mymod" is the namespace for your trait: this should be the same for all registries in your mod
    MyTrait = CharacterTrait.register("mymod:mytrait")
}
}}

== Trait script ==
You need to create a [[scripts]] which will define the trait properties. Create a [https://pz-wiki-modding.github.io/PZ-API-Docs/scripts/character_trait_definition.html character_trait_definition] script which will contain its name and cost as well as other important properties.
{{CodeSnip
| lang = java
| code =
module MyMod {
    character_trait_definition mymod:mytrait {
        /* identifier of the trait matching the one passed to CharacterTrait.register */
        CharacterTrait = mymod:mytrait,
        /* translation key of the trait's name */
        UIName = UI_trait_mymod:mytrait,
        /* translation key of the trait's description */
        UIDescription = UI_trait_mymod:mytrait_Desc,

        /* point cost of the trait */
        Cost = 2,

        /* if true, the trait will not be selectable at character creation */
        IsProfessionTrait = false,
        /* if true, the trait will not be selectable in multiplayer */
        DisabledInMultiplayer = false,
        
        /* list of recipes to give characters with the trait */
        GrantedRecipes = MakeSawPlank;SheetMetalWeapon,
        /* list of other traits granted when taking the trait */
        GrantedTraits = base:smoker,
        /* list of traits that cannot be taken at the same time as this trait */
        MutuallyExclusiveTraits = base:clumsy;base:deaf,
        /* list of skill levels to grant */
        XPBoosts = Aiming=1;Reloading=1,
    }
}
}}

{{Note|Make sure to check the [[PZ API Documentation|ScriptsDocs]] for what parameters are available for the [https://pz-wiki-modding.github.io/PZ-API-Docs/scripts/character_trait_definition.html character_trait_definition] script.}}

== Naming your trait ==
To name your trait, you need to reference the <code>UIName</code> value in your trait script into your mod's [https://pz-wiki-modding.github.io/PZ-API-Docs/translations/translation_files.html#ui UI.json] translation file where the key is the value of <code>UIName</code>. For the description, it is the same process but you need to reference the <code>UIDescription</code> value of your trait script. See [[translation]] for more information.
{{CodeSnip
| lang = json
| code =
{
    "UI_trait_mymod:mytrait": "My Trait name",
    "UI_trait_mymod:mytrait_Desc": "This is my trait description."
}
}}

== Giving your trait an icon ==
To give your trait an icon, you must place it inside of your media folder exactly like shown:
{{CodeSnip
| code =
📁 media/
    📁 ui/
        📁 Traits/
            📄 trait_mytrait.png    <--- mytrait here refers to the script ID mymod:mytrait, without "mymod:"
}}
Trait icons are rendered in 18x18 pixels. To create an icon for your trait it is recommended to use [https://www.gimp.org/ GIMP], or another image editor which supports 18x18.

== Referencing the trait in Lua ==
The script is enough for basic traits, but most trait mods will want to do something more complex than this. You will need to implement any custom functionality in [[Lua]]. 

Create a [[Lua (language)#Modules|module]] to store the registries from your mod. This should be in <code>media/lua/shared/</code> preferably and the relative path to the Lua file should be unique, which it is best to name after your mod.
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
---FILE: media/lua/shared/MyMod/registries.lua

-- name of the global table populated by media/registries.lua
return _MyModRegistries
}}

This module allows for an easy way to reference of your registries from other Lua scripts directly, instead of depending on a [[Lua (language)#Local and global|global variable]]. The reason we do that is to not poluate the global name space and for better code organization, but it is technically not necessary, just good practice.

This is how a trait that grants an item to the player when they first spawn would be implemented by referencing the trait from the registry module:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
---FILE: media/lua/server/MyMod/trait_on_spawn.lua

-- we need our registry to check for the trait
-- so we retrieve it and store it in a local variable for easy access
local MyModRegistries = require("MyMod/registries")

---this function will add a pistol to the player's inventory if they have the trait
---@param player IsoPlayer
local function addItemOnSpawn(player)
    -- check for the trait using the registry key
    if player:hasTrait(MyModRegistries.traits.MyTrait) then
        -- add a pistol to the player's inventory
        player:getInventory():addItem(ItemKey.Weapon.Pistol)

        -- in multiplayer, you may need to done syncing
    end
end

-- run our function when the OnNewGame event is triggered
-- contrary to the name, this event is triggered any time you create a new character, not just in new worlds
Events.OnNewGame.Add(addItemOnSpawn)
}}

{{Note|It is highly recommended to use [[Visual Studio Code]] alongside [[Umbrella (modding)|Umbrella]] to help with Lua modding.}}

== Adding a trait via Lua ==
An alternative to the [https://pz-wiki-modding.github.io/PZ-API-Docs/scripts/character_trait_definition.html character_trait_definition] script is to add the trait from Lua, for example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
local function createDynamicTrait()
    local traitName = getText("UI_trait_NightVision")
    local traitDesc = getText("UI_trait_NightVisionDesc")
    local myTrait = CharacterTraitDefinition.addCharacterTraitDefinition(
        CharacterTrait.NightVision,
        traitName,
        2,
        traitDesc,
        false,
        false
    )
end
Events.OnGameBoot.Add(createDynamicTrait)
}}

